Subject: [PATCH 0/2] HID/iio: Support the hinge sensor of Lenovo Yoga convertibles
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The ITE sensor hub (ITE8186, I2C HID) of the Lenovo Yoga 910-13IKB
exposes a custom sensor with the hinge, screen and keyboard angles. It is
the only tablet mode related signal of this convertible I could find
visible to the OS: the ACPI paths used by intel-vbtn, intel-hid and
soc_button_array are disabled by its firmware, and it lacks the WMI
interface of lenovo-ymc.

This sensor carries the same angles as the Intel ISH hinge sensor, so
this series routes it to the existing hid-sensor-custom-intel-hinge
driver, which learns a second layout:

 - patch 1 lets hid-sensor-custom recognise known custom sensors without
   a LUID, as this one only has a model property, and describes it as
   HID-SENSOR-LENOVO-020b;
 - patch 2 teaches the hinge driver the Lenovo layout.

Userspace sees the same "hinge" IIO device as on Intel machines: hinged,
an existing tablet mode daemon supporting the Intel hinge sensor, drives
it unmodified.

Other Lenovo Yoga models may use the same hub firmware, but I could only
test the 910-13IKB.

Testing, on a Yoga 910-13IKB (80VF, BIOS 2JCN39WW), with both modules
built out of tree against a 7.0 distribution kernel:

 - HID-SENSOR-LENOVO-020b is created and bound, and the "hinge" IIO
   device exposes the three labelled channels, with a scale of
   0.001745329 (a tenth of a degree) and a 10 Hz sampling frequency;
 - raw sysfs reads and the buffer both follow a full fold, from laptop
   to tablet and back, in agreement with the angle derived from the
   screen accelerometer, including while the base rests still;
 - through the generic hid-sensor-custom driver, the angles read back
   used to go stale while the base did not move. They no longer do once
   the IIO driver powers the sensor on through the usual power and
   reporting state sequence;
 - the Intel ISH path is untested, for lack of hardware. Its layout is
   unchanged, and its only functional change is how the capture callback
   reads fields narrower than 32 bits.

This series was checked with the help of an LLM coding assistant
(Claude, by Anthropic). It analysed the report descriptor and the driver
code, and ran the builds, checkpatch
and the hardware tests above on my machine, under my direction.

